Subject: [PATCH] agent: a radio's M1 re-sends for a lost M2 last until its M2: a new WSC exchange or a handled M2 restarts the budget, a spent round is followed by a new one, and no other command on the radio ends the exchange

0021 re-sends a radio's M1 while it waits in wsc_m2_pending, every third tick, at most eight
times, to solicit an M2 the controller sent once and that was lost. The count was reset only in
handle_autoconfig_resp, so the eight re-sends were one budget for the radio's whole life: every
Renew's exchange whose M2 came late used some of it. On rdk-1010 (21 October) a 3-radio
extender's 6 GHz radio spent six of the eight in one rejoin (seven M1s, the last answered). Once
the budget is gone, the next lost M2 leaves the radio in wsc_m2_pending for good: the agent then
answers no Topology Query (it waits for every radio) and drops that band's Renews, so the
controller retires it as silent and cannot bring it back; only an agent restart did.

reset_wsc_m2_recovery() restarts the counts whenever a new WSC exchange starts, for the
Autoconfig Response's M1 (as before) and now a Renew's M1 (handle_state_autoconfig_renew), and
when the M2 is handled (the radio leaves for owconfig_pending; a later re-arm by
rearm_onewifi_wsc_radio starts afresh).

When a round of eight is spent the radio no longer stops: it waits 30 ticks, doubling to 120
(about 2 minutes; while it waits the agent answers no Topology Query and the controller retires a
silent agent after 180 s), then starts a new round whose first M1 goes on that tick, each M1 a new
exchange with a fresh nonce and keys as the existing re-send already does. A restarted or
recovered controller answers the next M1 with an M2. The radio stays in wsc_m2_pending
throughout; the other radios are untouched (a dev_init would redo every radio).

The re-sends run from the radio's own protocol tick (em_t::proto_timeout, 0108), whatever command
the orchestrator holds, and the M1 is built from the radio's own data. What ended them was another
command. On rdk-1010 (21 October, a lone em_ctrl restart) the same extender's 6 GHz radio got the
Renew's M2, had its OneWifi subdoc queued behind another radio's and was re-armed (0105, 0111),
re-sent two M1s while its backhaul station was down, and then stopped: a sta_link_metrics command,
built for the radio because it has stations and runnable only on a configured radio, waited on it
for its 10 s time limit, and at the limit orch_transient set the radio, below
topo_synchronized, to unconfigured. 0109 and 0110 kept the WSC states only for dev_init's and
cfg_renew's time limits. Unconfigured, the radio left the re-send path for good, its agent
answered no Topology Query for the 20 minutes watched, and a new sta_link_metrics timed out on it
every 20 s. A command's time limit now keeps wsc_m2_pending and owconfig_pending whichever command
timed out. Its "Preserving" line is logged once for a radio and command type, then at most once
every 10 minutes with the count of such time limits since (wsc_kept_log_due): a command that
waits on the radio times out every few seconds for as long as the radio waits.

A command that runs on a radio in any state and sets its own state on it (ap_cap_query to
ap_cap_report, op_channel_report to channel_report_pending) ends the exchange the same way; an
ap_cap_query's time limit then left the radio at topo_synchronized, counted as configured with no
M2. While its exchange is in flight a radio now runs only a new exchange (dev_init, cfg_renew),
OneWifi's completion of its configuration (onewifi_cb) and the commands that leave its state as
they found it (sta_list, unassoc_sta_result); any other command waits for the radio or for its
time limit.

The round arithmetic is a static helper, wsc_m2_recovery_tick(), with unit tests
(test_l1_em_provisioning.cpp: the 30/60/120/120 waits, the first M1 on a round's tick, an unsent
M1 not spending the budget, the reset). test_l1_em_orch_agent.cpp runs the rounds as the agent
does, the orchestrator's handle_timeout and a test radio's proto_timeout each tick, the radio
counting the frames it sends: the radio's Renew command timing out with the radio still waiting,
another command timing out on it, another command coming for it, alone and with a configured
sibling radio (the sibling runs its part at once and sends nothing, the command ends for both at
its time limit), and the M2 landing then the radio re-armed. Each checks every re-send's tick
(rounds at the 30, 60 and 120 tick boundaries) and at most one M1 a tick; another test checks the
"Preserving" line's limit. Without the two orchestrator changes the two tests of another
command fail as the lab did: the radio left unconfigured after two re-sends, or held by the
ap_cap_query then at topo_synchronized after one; the Renew's and the M2's tests pass either way
(0108 and 0110 already kept those).
